  "query_preview": "/SATURDAY THROUGH THURSDAY/...\nIssued at 225 PM CDT Thu Oct 8 2026\n\nSaturday, ridging aloft will break down. With the ridge breakdown \nand a cold front dropping through the Black Hills Saturday \nafternoon, very warm air will reside across western and north \ncentral Nebraska. Another very warm day is expected across the \nforecast area. Southwesterly winds will be strong at 20 to 35 mph \nacross the east, and 10 to 20 mph across the western Sandhills. NBM \nforecast highs mainly 85 to 92. Highs could be a few degrees warmer \nthan this, supported by the latest MAV guidance. As for records \nSaturday, they are 88, 93, 94 and 95 degrees for Broken Bow, North \nPlatte, Imperial and Valentine respectively. \n\nSunday, the upper trough will persist across the West Coast, while \nupper ridging returns to the Southern and Central Plains. A weak \ncold front will move through the area. Highs temperatures will be \ncooler, yet still 5 to 15 degrees above normal, from the upper 60s \nto mid 70s northwest Sandhills to the upper 70s to low 80s (near 85 \nfar southwest). Winds will turn to the north and northeast in the \nafternoon 5 to 15 mph. \n\nSunday night into Monday, the surface front will stall across \nsouthern NE into central WY. Precipitation will remain off to our \nnorthwest across portions of WY and western SD. With the location of \nthe surface front, highs have trended warmer from the mid 60s far \nnorthern NE, to upper 60s and mid 70s (near 80 far southwest). A\nslight chance for showers Monday night across the northwest\nSandhills as a weak disturbance and cold front move into the \narea. The flow aloft will transition to southwesterly as a broad\ntrough of low pressure deepens across the western U.S Tuesday \ninto Wednesday. A shortwave trough may eject northeast across \nthe region Tuesday night into Wednesday with chance POPs. The \ndeterministic 12Z GFS shows western NE in the dry slot, to limit\nany chances for showers. The ECMWF remains more favorable, with\nthe shortwave trough slower, deeper and further east. The NBM \n24-hr probability of 0.10 inch or greater is from 40 to 50 \npercent. A slight warmup to the upper 60s to mid 70s Wednesday. \nA portion of the upper trough will push east across the Northern\nPlains Thursday, driving a cold front through western NE. Highs\ncooler from the upper 50s to mid 60s. Low chance POPS linger \ninto Wednesday night and Thursday.",
